  "prompt_text": "The figure uses two bridge-road cartoons to compare “early route” and “late route” measurements of the Hubble constant. Each yellow point with a short slanted error bar marks a published estimate, labeled by experiment or method. On the left, the early-universe route includes Planck, BBN+BAO, WMAP+BAO, ACTPol+BAO, and SPT-SZ+BAO. These points cluster near a lower value, around 67.4, with relatively small spread. On the right, the late-universe route includes local distance-ladder and related methods such as SH0ES, STRIDES, TRGB, Miras, Masers, H0LiCOW, and SBF. Those estimates sit noticeably higher, near 73.0, again with their own uncertainties.\n\nThe visual metaphor of two separate bridges over the same river emphasizes that two independent paths to the same quantity lead to different destinations. The road markings and small vertical scales suggest the disagreement is larger than the quoted error ranges for many of the measurements. Overall, the figure summarizes the “Hubble tension”: early-universe cosmology gives a lower expansion rate, while late-universe distance-based methods give a higher one.",
